WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.455
hello-devops keeps its visit count in Redis.

2
00:00:05.455 --> 00:00:08.836
So the web container has to reach the Redis container.

3
00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:12.209
A first try: set REDIS_HOST to localhost.

4
00:00:12.433 --> 00:00:15.968
But every container gets its own network namespace.

5
00:00:15.968 --> 00:00:20.404
Its own network card, its own IP address, and its own localhost.

6
00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:24.617
So inside a container, localhost means that container itself.

7
00:00:24.617 --> 00:00:28.796
The app calls itself, and nothing there listens on Redis's port.

8
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:35.516
Docker connects containers through a bridge, a virtual network switch inside the Linux VM.

9
00:00:35.733 --> 00:00:39.511
Each container gets a virtual cable, called a veth pair.

10
00:00:39.511 --> 00:00:42.007
One end is eth0 inside the container.

11
00:00:42.007 --> 00:00:44.436
The other end plugs into the bridge.

12
00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:52.703
Start a container without the network flag, and it joins this default network, called bridge.

13
00:00:52.703 --> 00:00:55.987
It gets an address such as 172.17.0.5.

14
00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:59.636
Now ask for Redis by name, from the web container.

15
00:00:59.636 --> 00:01:01.973
On the default bridge, that fails.

16
00:01:01.973 --> 00:01:02.798
Bad address.

17
00:01:02.798 --> 00:01:04.653
There is no lookup by name.

18
00:01:04.867 --> 00:01:06.815
By IP address, it works.

19
00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:10.600
But those addresses change when containers restart.

20
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:12.476
So you cannot hard-code them.

21
00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:17.386
And every container started without the network flag lands on this same bridge.

22
00:01:17.386 --> 00:01:19.817
So unrelated apps can talk to each other.

23
00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:23.827
Docker's own docs recommend a network you create yourself.

24
00:01:23.827 --> 00:01:25.331
A user-defined network.

25
00:01:25.331 --> 00:01:27.947
You make one with docker network create.

26
00:01:28.167 --> 00:01:30.347
Start both containers on hello-net.

27
00:01:30.347 --> 00:01:33.585
The other app is not on it, so it cannot reach them.

28
00:01:33.585 --> 00:01:35.952
Each app gets its own private network.

29
00:01:36.167 --> 00:01:42.256
Now each container gets a DNS server, at 127.0.0.11.

30
00:01:42.256 --> 00:01:45.652
Docker's embedded DNS server.

31
00:01:45.867 --> 00:01:48.391
The web app asks it: where is redis?

32
00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.745
It answers by name, with the current IP address of that container.

33
00:01:52.967 --> 00:01:56.361
Then the app connects to that address, over the network.

34
00:01:56.361 --> 00:01:57.695
Visits goes up by one.

35
00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:00.040
But how does the DNS server know?

36
00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:04.086
Docker Engine handed out those addresses in the first place.

37
00:02:04.086 --> 00:02:05.805
So it already has the list.

38
00:02:06.033 --> 00:02:12.355
Names it does not know, such as github.com, it passes on to the host's DNS servers.

39
00:02:13.067 --> 00:02:17.262
Containers on the same network reach each other on any port.

40
00:02:17.262 --> 00:02:19.011
Redis needs no -p at all.

41
00:02:19.233 --> 00:02:22.419
The -p flag is only for traffic from outside.

42
00:02:22.419 --> 00:02:27.375
The web container is published on port 8000, so your Mac can reach it.

43
00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:29.039
Now the data.

44
00:02:29.039 --> 00:02:32.145
Redis writes the count to its /data folder.

45
00:02:32.367 --> 00:02:36.876
Without a mount, that folder sits in the container's thin writable layer.

46
00:02:36.876 --> 00:02:40.273
And docker rm deletes that layer, with the count in it.

47
00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:48.677
So keep the data outside the container, in a volume: storage that Docker manages, which outlives the containers that use it.

48
00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:51.293
Mount it with -v redis-data:/data.

49
00:02:51.293 --> 00:02:53.756
The name on the left is the volume.

50
00:02:53.756 --> 00:02:57.133
The folder on the right is inside the container.

51
00:02:57.367 --> 00:02:58.490
Now swap Redis.

52
00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:02.685
Stop it with docker stop, so it can save its data first.

53
00:03:02.685 --> 00:03:03.808
Then remove it.

54
00:03:04.033 --> 00:03:05.303
The volume stays.

55
00:03:05.303 --> 00:03:08.740
Docker never deletes a named volume by itself.

56
00:03:08.967 --> 00:03:13.713
Start a brand-new Redis container, on the same network, with the same volume.

57
00:03:13.713 --> 00:03:16.425
Docker hands it an address, maybe a new one.

58
00:03:16.633 --> 00:03:20.916
The web app still asks for redis by name, and gets the new address.

59
00:03:20.916 --> 00:03:22.641
Nothing in the app changes.

60
00:03:22.867 --> 00:03:26.162
And the new Redis finds the old data in the volume.

61
00:03:26.162 --> 00:03:28.683
The count carries on, from five to six.

62
00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:33.547
A user-defined network gives containers names, through Docker's DNS.

63
00:03:33.547 --> 00:03:36.828
A volume keeps the data when the container goes.
